insert into activities (id, lesson_id, position, type, concept, points, counts_toward_unlock, content) values
  ('8c4326ce-d083-59c2-ac41-ccb363148a96', '5580fd8d-6f89-59c9-ac7f-ebda6837f46a', 2, 'content', 'computer parts', 0, true,
   '{"markdown":"## How a computer processes information\n\nEvery computer has four kinds of parts. Information moves between them.\n\n- **Input** brings information in. Examples: keyboard, mouse, microphone, touch screen.\n- **Processing** follows the program''s instructions. This is the **CPU**, sometimes called the computer''s \"brain\".\n- **Memory and storage** hold data and programs. **RAM** is short-term memory for what you are using right now. An **SSD** or hard drive keeps your files even when the power is off.\n- **Output** shows or plays the result. Examples: screen, speaker, printer.\n\nThe CPU trades information with memory and storage all the time while it works.\n\n### Example: typing and saving your work\n\n1. You press keys on the keyboard (input).\n2. The CPU follows the word processor''s instructions (processing).\n3. Your words sit in RAM while you type (memory).\n4. The letters appear on the screen (output).\n5. When you click Save, the file is written to the SSD (storage), so it is still there tomorrow.\n\n### Everything is 0s and 1s\n\nInside, a computer turns all information into **binary**: long patterns of 0s and 1s.\nEach 0 or 1 is called a **bit**.\nLetters, colors, sounds and videos are all stored as bits.\n\n**Big idea:** a program tells the computer how to use the data.","title":"How a computer processes information"}'::jsonb)
