insert into activities (id, lesson_id, position, type, concept, points, counts_toward_unlock, content) values
  ('856d478f-d629-5560-9f89-5826f98981ed', 'a4576df6-76de-548b-b906-b1040b033f3d', 1, 'content', 'early vs modern computers', 0, true,
   '{"markdown":"## Early computers\n\nWhat do you think the oldest computer looked like? It did not have a screen or a keyboard.\n\n### The Difference Engine\n\nIn **1822**, an English inventor named **Charles Babbage** proposed a machine called the **Difference Engine**.\nIt was a **mechanical calculator**. Instead of electronic circuits, it used gears and wheels to work out tables of numbers.\n\nIt could only do one kind of job: calculating. It was not a general-purpose computer like a laptop, which can run many different programs.\n\nBabbage never finished building his machines. Much later, a museum built his design for the **Difference Engine No. 2**.\nThat finished machine weighs about five tons!\n\n### Think of a coin sorter\n\nA coin sorter helps explain the Difference Engine. Both machines:\n\n1. Take in an **input** (for a coin sorter, a pile of mixed coins)\n2. **Process** it mechanically, with moving parts\n3. Give an **output** in the end (sorted coins, or a table of answers)\n\nInput, processing, output: the same pattern you saw in modern computers.","title":"Early computers"}'::jsonb)
